WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.381
Hi, I'm Kilter!

00:00:02.381 --> 00:00:07.470
Resume training on a different number of GPUs, and most loaders feed different data.

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:08.197
Zephon doesn't.

00:00:09.517 --> 00:00:13.064
The idea: a fixed number of lanes, each with its own state,

00:00:13.064 --> 00:00:14.542
owned by GPUs in blocks.

00:00:19.317 --> 00:00:23.440
A usual loader deals documents to GPUs, each packing its own rows.

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:26.188
Change the GPU count, and the rows change.

00:00:26.608 --> 00:00:28.301
Zephon deals documents to lanes.

00:00:28.301 --> 00:00:31.009
A GPU only picks where a lane runs.

00:00:32.717 --> 00:00:36.252
A work source plans chunks of sample pointers, and a seeded shuffle

00:00:36.252 --> 00:00:37.430
deals them to lanes.

00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:41.706
Each lane tokenizes, packs and shuffles with its own bins and buffers.

00:00:42.126 --> 00:00:44.165
GPUs own lanes in blocks.

00:00:44.165 --> 00:00:47.428
Sixty-four lanes on sixteen GPUs is four each.

00:00:47.848 --> 00:00:50.301
Every step takes the same slice from every lane.

00:00:52.108 --> 00:00:53.232
Here is the formula.

00:00:54.108 --> 00:00:58.512
The GPU count divides the lane count, so every GPU owns the same number.

00:00:58.932 --> 00:01:02.739
The lanes divide the step's microbatches, so each lane gives whole ones.

00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:09.011
In the paper's run a step is sixty-four microbatches, so only sixty-four

00:01:09.011 --> 00:01:11.241
lanes fits eight to sixty-four GPUs.

00:01:13.733 --> 00:01:16.952
Resuming needs the packing state, which Zephon never saves.

00:01:17.372 --> 00:01:20.312
It saves a cursor per lane, plus the chunks still in flight.

00:01:20.732 --> 00:01:24.022
Every few chunks, a flush empties and resets every bin.

00:01:24.442 --> 00:01:27.819
On resume it replays from the last flush, rebuilding the bins.

00:01:28.239 --> 00:01:29.712
Then it drops what was delivered.

00:01:32.008 --> 00:01:35.404
With a standard loader, resumed runs split after the fault, and

00:01:35.404 --> 00:01:36.639
the hashes turn red.

00:01:37.059 --> 00:01:40.371
With Zephon, one curve carries on, and every hash matches.

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:44.152
Zephon fixes the data, not the arithmetic.

00:01:44.152 --> 00:01:48.163
Summed over more GPUs, the same batch rounds a little differently.

00:01:50.025 --> 00:01:51.355
Lanes carry the data.

00:01:51.355 --> 00:01:52.685
GPUs just run them.

00:01:54.625 --> 00:01:56.787
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:56.787 --> 00:01:57.405
I'm Kilter.

00:01:57.405 --> 00:01:57.714
Bye!
